  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 AM CDT Sun Jul 26 2026\n\nWell the first thing we'll really need to evaluate is the smoke \npotential with all the activity across the west. A trough develops \nacross the western Dakotas later today, with a plume headed into \nmainly western to central/north central South Dakota and potentially \nhaving impacts to visibility. The HRRR shows less smoke moving in at \nground level across the east. Aloft, smoke continues but will be \nmost prominent Monday and is likely to have an impact on \ntemperatures. \n\nRegarding the heat today, NBM dewpoints are still forecast close to \n70. Looking deeper, the NBM still has a GEFS issue which is overly \nmixy, and artificially weighing down dewpoints, while the EC and \nCanadian ensembles picked up on the 'well into 70s' dewpoints that we \nended up experiencing yesterday. EC members are a little lower \ntoday, while the Canadian members are on par compared to Saturday. \nAssuming a low bias in NBM dewpoints, and temperatures still maybe \njust a few degrees too warm temperature-wise east river but on \ntarget for the Missouri valley... there is enough evidence for an \nupgrade to the Advisory to a Heat Warning.   \n\nThe upper pattern remains stagnant through mid-week with the 4-\nCorners high and a northwest flow regime east of the ridge axis that \npersists along the WY/MT and Dakotas state line. The next plume of \nmoisture that pivots around  the high and into the Northern Plains \ncomes in late Monday/Tuesday timeframe. A wave coming in off the \nPacific eventually suppresses the high and gives us our next, best \nchance for moisture Wednesday night/Thursday. Otherwise, peak heat \ncontinues for now today, with some slight diminishment but still  5 \nto 15 degrees above normal for the first half of the work week.",
